 { q: 'How does screen printing work step by step?', a: 'A digital design is separated by color and prepared as screens. Ink is applied through the screens, then cured under the selected ink manufacturer\'s documented time, temperature, equipment, garment, and process requirements.' },
 { q: 'Is screen printing better than embroidery?', a: 'For tees, hoodies, and large/colorful designs at volume — yes. Screen printing prints flat onto fabric so big designs and gradients work, and per-piece cost falls fast at quantity because most cost is in setup. Embroidery wins on polos, button-downs, hats, and any garment where a premium textured look matters more than design size or color.' },
 { q: 'What fabrics work with screen printing?', a: 'Compatibility depends on the exact garment, fabric, coating, ink system, preparation, cure, artwork, and production partner. Request product-specific approval and written minimums.' },
